<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Your base is not a spot on the court, which is why "recover to the middle" keeps getting you caught out.</strong> BWF defines base as the position from which you can best deal with your opponent's probable replies, and calls it a fluid position rather than a set point. In doubles that means it moves with your pair's shape: further forward when you are attacking, wider and deeper when you are defending. Split step from wherever that is, taking off just before they hit it and landing as or after they do. The corners stay put. Where you start from does not.</p>

      <p>You see the smash coming, you have time to think about it, and your feet are still flat on the floor when it goes past you.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>Base moves.</strong> It is wherever you can best cover the likely reply, not a fixed point behind the service line. Most footwork guides get this wrong for doubles.</li>
        <li><strong>Take off before they hit it, land as or after they do.</strong> The split step straddles their contact. If you are still going up as the shuttle leaves, you are late.</li>
        <li><strong>It is a shallow drop, not a jump.</strong> Badminton Insight puts the ceiling at about the width of a racket grip.</li>
        <li><strong>The foot that lands first decides where you go.</strong> Left first to move right, right first to move left, front first to move back.</li>
        <li><strong>There is no all-purpose split step.</strong> BWF says so directly, and in doubles it matters more, because you are covering an asymmetric half rather than a symmetric court.</li>
        <li><strong>You lunge far less than a singles player.</strong> At elite level roughly a third as often, which is worth knowing before you spend a winter drilling lunges.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for UK club-level doubles players who have never had formal coaching. Technique here is attributed to the BWF coaching syllabus and to named coaches; we do not teach it in our own voice. Every published figure comes from elite play, because no study of club-level movement exists.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#late">Why am I always arriving late?</a>
      <a href="#what">What is a split step, and when exactly?</a>
      <a href="#does-it-work">Does it actually work?</a>
      <a href="#base">Where should you be standing when they hit it?</a>
      <a href="#six">Are there really six corners?</a>
      <a href="#which">Which corners will you actually use?</a>
      <a href="#front">Getting to the two front corners</a>
      <a href="#back">Getting to the back two</a>
      <a href="#middle">The middle two, which only exist in doubles</a>
      <a href="#foot">Which foot leads?</a>
      <a href="#knee">Landing a lunge without wrecking your knee</a>
      <a href="#after">Where do you go after you hit?</a>
      <a href="#every-shot">Do you split step on every shot?</a>
      <a href="#fitter">Do you have to get fitter?</a>
      <a href="#practise">How do you practise this at a club night?</a>
      <a href="#natural">How long before it feels natural?</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="late">Why am I always arriving late?</h2>
      <p>You are starting to move when the shuttle leaves their racket, and by then you are already about a quarter of a second behind.</p>
      <p>Watching the shuttle and then reacting feels like the right thing to do. It is what everyone does before somebody tells them otherwise. The problem is that the information you need arrives before contact, not after it, and a player standing flat-footed has to overcome inertia before they can use it.</p>
      <p>What good players do instead is arrive at a state of readiness a fraction before their opponent hits, so that the first movement is a redirection rather than a start. Everything else in this guide is detail on that one idea.</p>

      <h2 id="what">What is a split step, and when exactly?</h2>
      <p>A split step is a shallow drop onto both feet, taken off just before your opponent strikes the shuttle and landing as or just after they do.</p>
      <p>BWF's Level 1 coaching manual describes it as a shallow jump off the ground just before the opponent strikes, with the landing occurring with or after the opponent hitting the shuttle. Badminton Insight, run by former England internationals Greg and Jenny Mairs, gives the same window in club-player language: initiate just before they hit, push off just after contact once the direction is known.</p>
      <p><strong>The timing straddles their contact.</strong> That is the part club players get wrong, and it is why a diagram helps more than a sentence.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/split-step-timing.svg" alt="A timeline strip showing the opponent's swing above and your feet below. Reading left to right: the opponent begins their backswing, then their forward swing; you take off into a shallow drop before their racket reaches the shuttle; their racket contacts the shuttle; you land at or just after that contact, with both feet down and knees soft; and only then do you push off in the direction the shuttle is going. Below, a second strip shows the common club mistake, where the player is still standing flat at contact and only begins to move after the shuttle has left the racket, arriving late by roughly the time it takes to overcome standing inertia." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Take-off before contact, landing at or after it. If you are still rising as the shuttle leaves the racket, the split step has cost you time rather than saved it.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p style="margin-top:22px"><strong>It is not a jump.</strong> Badminton Insight puts the ceiling plainly: hopping any higher than the width of a racket grip is inefficient. BWF calls it a shallow jump. The commonest club error, once someone has been told about split steps at all, is a visible hop that leaves them in the air when they need to be pushing off the floor.</p>

      <h2 id="does-it-work">Does it actually work?</h2>
      <p>The timing rule is well-attested coaching doctrine, the mechanism is a reasonable inference, and a measured speed benefit in badminton has not been demonstrated - and we would rather tell you that than pretend otherwise.</p>
      <p>Here is the evidence as it actually stands. The instruction itself is solid: it comes from the world governing body's coaching syllabus and is taught identically by credentialed coaches, which is about as good as coaching guidance gets.</p>
      <p>The usual explanation is that dropping into a slight crouch pre-stretches the leg muscles so they produce force more quickly. That is real physiology, and BWF does invoke pre-stretch in its manual - but it does so in the section on <em>jumping</em>, not in its description of the split step. Calling it the reason the split step works is an inference, and we are marking it as one.</p>
      <p><strong>The one badminton-specific study we could find does not show what a coaching article would want it to show.</strong> It measured joint angles and how the landing loads the leg, and reported that the split step changed the loading characteristics of the lunge that followed. It reported no measure of players reaching the shuttle sooner. A higher loading rate is, in most of the movement literature, discussed in terms of injury risk rather than performance.</p>
      <p>The precise timing figures that circulate come from tennis, where the equivalent has been studied more. That work is consistent with the badminton coaching window, but it is tennis, and a different sport's finding is not evidence for this one. If you have seen a specific percentage quoted for how much a split step improves reaction, we could not trace it to any study.</p>

      <h2 id="base">Where should you be standing when they hit it?</h2>
      <p>Stand wherever you can best cover their most likely reply, which in doubles is a different place depending on whether your pair is attacking or defending.</p>
      <p>This is the correction that reorganises everything else. BWF's manual defines base as the court position from which you are best able to deal with your opponent's probable replies, and states explicitly that the base is a fluid position, not a set point on the court, and that it is where the player performs a split step timed with the opponent striking the shuttle.</p>
      <p>Read that definition carefully and it is about the opponent, not about the court. It never mentions the middle. So it is already true in doubles without any adaptation, and it quietly contradicts the most widely repeated instruction in footwork content.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/doubles-base-moves.svg" alt="Three small badminton courts side by side, each showing where one player splits from in a different situation. In the first, the pair is attacking in a front-and-back shape, and the front player's base is close to the net and slightly to one side while the rear player's base is deep. In the second, the pair is defending side by side, and both bases are wider and further back, level with each other across the court. In the third, a flat exchange, both bases are mid-court and closer together. In all three the six corners of the court stay in exactly the same places; only the base positions move." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The corners never move. Your base does, because it is set by what your pair is doing rather than by the geometry of the court.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p style="margin-top:22px">There is a second consequence, and BWF states it directly: there is no split step that will let a player move off equally in all directions, and with experience players learn to land in a way that covers the most likely replies. In singles you are covering a symmetric court, so that is a refinement. In doubles you are covering an asymmetric half whose shape your partner determines, so the landing has to be biased every single time.</p>
      <p>Where your pair stands as a unit is a separate subject with its own answers. If the shapes above are unfamiliar, <a href="/guides/badminton-doubles-positioning">our guide to doubles positioning</a> covers them properly, and this guide will make more sense afterwards.</p>

      <h2 id="six">Are there really six corners?</h2>
      <p>The governing body teaches four, the popular model teaches six, and for a doubles player the six-corner version is the more useful of the two.</p>
      <p>BWF's Level 1 manual publishes four movement patterns: forehand and backhand forecourt, forehand and backhand rearcourt. Badminton Insight teaches four as well, naming the rear non-racket-side corner "round the head" rather than backhand, which is a more accurate description of what you actually do there.</p>
      <p>The six-corner model adds mid-left and mid-right. Those two are usually presented as an extra pair of destinations, and for a singles player they are close to redundant, because you pass through the midcourt rather than playing from it.</p>
      <p><strong>For a doubles player they are the opposite of redundant, because side-by-side defence lives there.</strong> When your pair is defending, you are not travelling through the midcourt on your way somewhere: you are standing in it, pushing sideways, and playing shots from it. That is a real position with its own movement, and the four-corner model has nowhere to put it.</p>
      <p>So we teach six, and we would note that the credentialed sources teaching four are teaching a foundation that is singles-shaped rather than being wrong.</p>

      <h2 id="which">Which corners will you actually use?</h2>
      <p>Far more of your game happens at the front and through the middle than at the back, which is the reverse of how most footwork content allocates its attention.</p>
      <p>One published figure makes the point sharply. A study of the final stages of the 2016 European Championships counted movements per player per match, and found singles players performing about 158 to 175 lunges against about 47 in doubles. A singles player lunges roughly three times as often as a doubles player does.</p>
      <p>That is worth sitting with, because the lunge is the most-taught element of six-corner footwork and it is the element your format uses least.</p>
      <p><strong>The same study counted where on court each shot was struck</strong>, and we have worked its counts into shares of a pair's total shots. The figures below are our arithmetic on the authors' table rather than something they state:</p>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:16px">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.95rem">
          <caption style="caption-side:top;text-align:left;padding-bottom:8px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Share of a pair's shots by distance from the net. Zones are the study's own, and they are not equal in size.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line)">
              <th style="padding:8px 10px">Zone (from the net)</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Men's doubles</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Women's doubles</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Mixed</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Net, to the short service line (198 cm)</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">23.9%</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">17.6%</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">24.9%</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Front, next 198 cm</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">38.5%</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">30.7%</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">38.3%</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Middle, next 198 cm</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">29.8%</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">35.0%</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">26.6%</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:8px 10px"><strong>Rear, the last 76 cm before the baseline</strong></td><td style="padding:8px 10px"><strong>7.8%</strong></td><td style="padding:8px 10px"><strong>16.6%</strong></td><td style="padding:8px 10px"><strong>10.2%</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Read that bottom row carefully, because it is easy to over-read.</strong> The study's "rear" zone is not the rearcourt as you would use the word. It is the 76 centimetre strip between the doubles long service line and the baseline, about a ninth of the half court's depth. Most of what you would call playing from the back falls inside the study's middle zone.</p>
      <p>So the honest version is narrower than it first looks, and still useful: elite doubles players almost never strike the shuttle from right on the back tramline. The authors themselves group rear and middle together as the rear half of the court, which for men's doubles comes to about 38 per cent of shots.</p>
      <p>Two limits worth stating. This is seven matches per event at a European championship, played by ranked internationals, and there is no equivalent study of club play at all. And a jump smash is counted where the player <em>lands</em>, so a smash struck from the back and landed forward of the service line counts as a middle-zone shot.</p>
      <p><strong>What follows practically:</strong> if you have one evening a month of attention to spend on movement, spend it on the forecourt and the sideways push, not on drilling deep lunges into the back tramline.</p>

      <h2 id="front">Getting to the two front corners</h2>
      <p>Split, chassé, lunge, and chassé back - and keep the chassé short, because it is a slow way to cover distance.</p>
      <p>BWF's published pattern for the forehand forecourt is exactly that sequence, and its coaching notes on the chassé are worth quoting because they are specific: one foot chases the other but never quite catches it, and players should be encouraged to keep ground contact times short, as though the floor is hot.</p>
      <p>The manual also warns that using the chassé for long distances, such as the diagonals of the court, is slow and should be discouraged. So the chassé is a short-range adjustment, not a way of travelling. If you are chasséing a long way, you should be running.</p>
      <p>These are the corners your doubles game uses most. If any single movement is worth practising, it is this one.</p>

      <h2 id="back">Getting to the back two</h2>
      <p>Split, then pivot or hop around your non-racket leg, then jump and turn to hit while off the ground - or run backwards on a curved path if you have time.</p>
      <p>BWF gives both. For the backhand rearcourt the pattern is the split step, a pivot or hop around the non-racket leg, then a jump and turn in the air striking the shuttle while off the ground, with the note that the pivot should cover some distance so it helps carry the player into the corner.</p>
      <p>The alternative is a split step followed by backwards running steps, and the manual specifies that those steps should follow a slightly curved path, which helps the player get side-on to the shuttle. The instinct is to run straight back, and straight back leaves you square to the net with no shot.</p>
      <p>For the forehand rearcourt, BWF gives a split step, a cross-behind, and a lunge, and notes that this tends to be a straight-line movement into the corner. It also observes that it is very rare for more than one cross-behind to be performed at a time, which is a useful check on anyone who has been taught to shuffle backwards repeatedly.</p>

      <h2 id="middle">The middle two, which only exist in doubles</h2>
      <p>Push sideways from a wider, lower base rather than lunging forward, because defending is a lateral action and not a reaching one.</p>
      <p>This is the section that no singles-framed footwork guide contains, and it is where the six-corner model earns its place. When your pair is defending side by side, you are standing in the midcourt with your weight low and your base wider than it would be anywhere else on court, covering a smash that will arrive faster than you can plan for.</p>
      <p>The movement is a lateral push off the outside foot, keeping the racket up and in front. It is not a lunge, and treating it as one is what leaves club players stretching at full extension for a shuttle they could have taken comfortably by moving their feet twenty centimetres.</p>
      <p>Which side you cover and how the pair splits the court between you belongs to positioning rather than footwork, and <a href="/guides/badminton-doubles-positioning">the positioning guide</a> handles it.</p>

      <h2 id="foot">Which foot leads?</h2>
      <p>On the split step the foot that lands first determines the direction you move; in the lunge it is almost always your racket leg.</p>
      <p>These are two different rules and they get conflated constantly. The split-step rule is the one almost nobody publishes, and BWF states it directly: coaching it as a two-footed landing is reasonable, but in reality one foot nearly always lands before the other, and the foot that lands first governs which direction the player moves off.</p>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:16px">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.95rem">
          <thead>
            <tr style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line)">
              <th style="padding:8px 10px">Foot that lands first</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Direction you move</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Left</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">To the right</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Right</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">To the left</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Front</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">Backwards</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:8px 10px">Back</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">Forwards</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">The lunge rule is simpler. Badminton Insight teaches lunging on your racket leg - right leg for a right-hander, left for a left-hander - and BWF agrees with a qualifier, noting that most lunging is done on the racket leg but some non-racket-leg lunging does happen.</p>

      <h2 id="knee">Landing a lunge without wrecking your knee</h2>
      <p>Point your lead foot, knee and contact direction the same way, turn out the rear foot, and bend the rear knee - and those coaching points exist to protect the joint rather than to look tidy.</p>
      <p>BWF's lunge coaching points are unusually explicit about why they exist. The lead foot points towards the shuttle; the lead leg, knee and point of contact face the same direction to protect the knee and ankle joints; the rear foot is turned out to help with balance and range of movement; the rear knee bends to reduce strain; and the rear arm extends for balance.</p>
      <p>The knee and ankle are the two most commonly injured joints in badminton, and the lunge is where the loading happens. This is the one part of the guide we would ask you to read twice, because adult club players who take up footwork drills without this tend to lunge deeper than they used to, on a knee that is not tracking over the foot.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/lunge-knee-tracking.svg" alt="Two front-on views of a player landing a lunge. On the left, marked as safe: the lead foot points towards the shuttle, and a vertical line shows the lead knee tracking directly over that foot in the same direction; the rear foot is turned out and the rear knee is bent, with the rear arm extended for balance. On the right, marked as the pattern to avoid: the lead foot points one way while the knee collapses inward across it, so the knee and foot no longer face the same direction, and the rear leg is straight with the foot square rather than turned out." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Lead foot, lead knee and the direction you are hitting all face the same way. BWF's coaching points for the lunge are framed as joint protection, and this is the one they mean.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="after">Where do you go after you hit?</h2>
      <p>Go to wherever your next base is, which is often not where you came from and is rarely the middle.</p>
      <p>"Recover to base after every shot" is the standard instruction on the popular six-corner pages, and it sits awkwardly against BWF calling base a fluid position rather than a set point. Badminton Insight goes further and lists unnecessary returns to base among the common split-step mistakes.</p>
      <p>In doubles the tension is sharpest, because your pair's shape may have changed while you were playing the shot. If you have just lifted from the net, your pair is now defending and your base is wider and deeper than the one you left. Travelling back to where you started would be travelling to a base that no longer exists.</p>
      <p><strong>We should be straight that the sources disagree here</strong>, and no authority settles it. What we would take from it: move to cover the reply you have just invited, rather than to a location.</p>

      <h2 id="every-shot">Do you split step on every shot?</h2>
      <p>Nearly always yes, and the honest exception is a smash coming straight at your body, where coaches disagree about whether there is time.</p>
      <p>The argument for skipping it is that when the shuttle is already travelling at you there is nothing to redirect towards, so a split step spends time you do not have. The argument against is that the split step is what gets your weight off your heels, which is exactly what body defence needs.</p>
      <p>We could not find an authority that settles this, and half of club doubles is defending, so it matters more to your game than it would to a singles player's. If you want a working rule: keep the split step, make it smaller and earlier when you expect a smash, and land wider than usual.</p>

      <h2 id="fitter">Do you have to get fitter?</h2>
      <p>No, and that is most of the appeal - footwork is a positional gain rather than an aerobic one.</p>
      <p>Moving earlier and in fewer steps means you cover the same court with less running. It will not make a long rally feel easier, and anyone telling you that better footwork substitutes for fitness is overselling it. What it does is get you to more shuttles at the fitness you already have.</p>
      <p>The lunge figures above are relevant here too. Doubles is not the format where the movement volume sits, so a club doubles player worried about the physical demands of learning this is worrying about the wrong thing.</p>

      <h2 id="practise">How do you practise this at a club night?</h2>
      <p>Shadow the movements without a shuttle, and use the hall's own lines to time your split steps.</p>
      <p>BWF's manual gives a split-step drill that needs no partner and no shuttle: run down the sports hall performing a split step at given intervals, using the lines to guide them. That is a thing you can do in the ten minutes before anyone else arrives.</p>
      <p>Its teaching sequence is also usable on yourself. The manual describes a whole-part-whole method with footwork as its worked example: try the whole movement into the forehand forecourt, isolate the chassé and practise that alone, then put the whole movement back together.</p>
      <p><strong>An honest note on the evidence.</strong> Shadow footwork is recommended universally by coaching sources, and we could find no study evaluating whether it works or how much of it is useful. The one peer-reviewed review of badminton footwork training we located does not cover it, give durations, or address doubles at all. Treat it as established practice rather than as something measured.</p>

      <h2 id="natural">How long before it feels natural?</h2>
      <p>Nobody has measured this, and we are not going to invent a number for it.</p>
      <p>We looked for a figure and there is not one: no study, no coaching consensus, not even a commonly repeated rule of thumb. That is a genuine gap in what is published about a topic that is otherwise well covered.</p>
      <p>What we can say is what the coaching sources describe: the split step is the part that becomes automatic soonest because it repeats every rally, and the movement patterns to specific corners take longer because they come up less often.</p>
      <p><strong>And the concession this guide owes you.</strong> Footwork is probably not the best use of your next hour. If your games are one-sided, or you and your partner keep ending up in the same half of the court, those cost you far more points than your movement does and they are quicker to fix. Come back to this when the rest is working.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Drop onto both feet just before your opponent hits the shuttle, every rally, wherever you happen to be standing. Nothing else here works without it.</p>
      </div>

